{"kind":"propose","text":"MULTI-RESIDENT COORDINATION: DIRECTION PROPOSAL (phase 1 of assignment 78963842, P1 backlog item 30218c6e...85fe10f1)\n\nRecommendation in one line: take option (a), a per-repository exclusive claim carried by the advertisement file itself, where a second resident refuses to serve before it binds anything, and stale-owner recovery is decided by probing the advertised address rather than by checking a PID. Option (b), a shared nexus or leader design, is rejected as disproportionate: it contains option (a) as a subproblem and buys nothing the adopted supported-host boundary makes valuable.\n\n== 1. THE PROBLEM ==\n\nTwo residents for one repository do not corrupt the durable log, but they split leased presence and ephemeral conversation into two rooms whose participants cannot see each other and are never told. A second resident must not silently start an independent ephemeral room. The behaviour is already documented as the case to avoid, with no enforcement, at docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md:108-119 and docs/reference/gs/serve.md:121-129: \"Run exactly one. Nothing enforces it: there is no lock.\"\n\n== 2. CURRENT LIFECYCLE, AS THE CODE HAS IT ==\n\nStartup. serveCommand is cmd/gs/main.go:1654-1719. It parses --listen, defaulting to 127.0.0.1:7777 (main.go:1656); refuses a non-loopback address (main.go:1662, validateLoopbackListen at main.go:1747-1760); refuses a read-only attachment (main.go:1669-1671); builds the server (main.go:1677); binds (main.go:1689); then publishes (main.go:1694). The order is deliberate and commented at main.go:1686-1688: bind first so the advertised address is the one actually served, including the port the kernel chose for :0.\n\nAdvertisement. internal/app/resident.go. The record is Resident{URL, Genesis, PID} (resident.go:15-19), written as a 0600 temp file and renamed into place (resident.go:33-40). It lives at MetaDir/resident.json, and MetaDir is filepath.Join(commonDir, \"gitseq\") (internal/app/app.go:351), where commonDir comes from git rev-parse --git-common-dir (app.go:203-217). So every linked worktree of one repository shares one advertisement, and git — not gitseq — does the path canonicalisation.\n\nThe doc comment states the defect exactly (resident.go:23-26): \"It is not a lock: the last writer wins, and a record left behind by a dead process costs a client only a refused connection before it falls back to acting locally.\" PublishResident never reads the existing record before overwriting it.\n\nReading. ResidentURL (resident.go:55-61) accepts a record only when the URL is non-empty and the genesis matches the workspace. It performs no liveness check, so a record left by a killed process is returned as valid. The one non-test consumer is the MCP adapter, in room.endpoint() at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:148-167; the gs CLI itself never auto-discovers and requires an explicit --server.\n\nWithdrawal. defer withdraw() at main.go:1698; the closure removes the file only when the record's PID still equals ours (resident.go:41-48), so a clean shutdown does not delete a successor's record. Only SIGINT and SIGTERM are trapped (main.go:71, watcher at main.go:1705-1711). On SIGKILL nothing runs and the record survives with a dead PID.\n\nThe room. The nexus is per-process and in memory. internal/service/server.go:63-68 gives the Server sole ownership of one *nexus.Hub, constructed at server.go:77 by nexus.New(4096). The package describes itself as \"an intentionally amnesiac collaboration rendezvous. Its cursor namespace dies with the process\" (internal/nexus/nexus.go:1-3). Each Hub generates a fresh ed25519 signing key (nexus.go:243-249) and a fresh random generation identifier (nexus.go:253-276), and holds history, presence, conversations, participants and inboxes in plain maps behind one mutex (nexus.go:225-241). Sessions are leases with an expiry (presenceEntry, nexus.go:208-223).\n\nDurable appends. Sequencing is a compare-and-swap loop on the git ref: kernel.go:480-541, with store.UpdateRef(ctx, ref, commit, head) at kernel.go:523 (git update-ref ref new old, internal/gitstore/gitstore.go:287), a retry when the head moved under the attempt (kernel.go:523-527), and dedup-key replay so a repeated act is not double-appended (kernel.go:499-505). Two residents appending concurrently is therefore already correct. This is the invariant to preserve untouched, and nothing proposed below goes near it.\n\n== 3. WHAT HAPPENS TODAY WHEN A SECOND RESIDENT STARTS ==\n\nIt depends entirely on the listen address, which is the whole defect.\n\nSame address: net.Listen fails at main.go:1689 with \"address already in use\", the error returns before anything is published, and the incumbent's record is untouched. The default is a fixed port, so the common accident is already caught — by the operating system, not by gitseq, and only for that one address.\n\nAny other address (--listen 127.0.0.1:0, another port, or another loopback IP such as 127.0.0.2:7777): the bind succeeds, and PublishResident overwrites the incumbent's record with an atomic rename. There is no refusal, no warning, and no trace that a takeover happened. Two live residents now serve one repository:\n\n- Their rooms are disjoint. Presence, conversations and inboxes are separate maps in separate processes, and the frames of each are signed by a different ed25519 key generated at nexus.New.\n- Clients that read the advertisement follow the newest record. The MCP adapter re-reads it whenever its cached endpoint is lost (cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:172-178).\n- A client carrying a session from the old room is refused by the new one: Observe returns \"session is not present\" (nexus.go:689-697). The MCP adapter treats that as a resident restart and silently re-announces once (cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:1235-1245). It therefore recovers by migrating into the other room without telling anybody, while its presence in the first room lingers until the lease expires.\n- A client carrying only a cursor gets a generation mismatch, which returns ErrReset (nexus.go:788-792) and sets Reset on the observation (nexus.go:704-706). It re-snapshots into the other room.\n\nThat last pair is the sharpest finding, and it is the reason the existing generation mechanism does not already solve this: a takeover and an ordinary restart are indistinguishable to every client. \"Reset\" and \"session is not present\" are exactly what a restarted resident says. Nothing in the protocol can express \"you are now talking to a different resident that has been running in parallel all along\", so the split is not merely unenforced — it is unobservable from the client side. Anyone left holding the first resident's URL explicitly, via --server, stays in the abandoned room indefinitely and sees a board that is quietly wrong.\n\n== 4. HOW THE ADOPTED SUPPORTED-HOST DECISION BEARS ON THIS ==\n\nThe ratified supported-host decision (proposal 8a758fb795b459f78832efa3ca9aee02c226a6d2, ratified by bc758b7a614ce25041485a3544321afa50836bbb) says: \"support the signing resident only where every process that can reach it or read its repository key storage is trusted as the same operator. Do not claim shared-host or mutually untrusted local-process support.\" It also fixes the posture for this kind of gap: the boundary \"must be explicit and fail closed\".\n\nTwo consequences shape the design space.\n\nFirst, a second resident is an operator mistake, not an adversary. Every local process is stipulated to be the same operator. There are no mutually distrusting parties whose competing claims need arbitration, so the coordination problem is degenerate: it needs a decision, not a protocol. This is what makes option (b) expensive and pointless at the same time.\n\nSecond, fail closed is the adopted house style for exactly this shape of unenforced assumption, so refusing to start is the answer already sanctioned rather than a new policy invented here.\n\n== 5. OPTION (a): PER-REPOSITORY EXCLUSIVE CLAIM ==\n\nWhere the claim lives. In resident.json itself, not in a second lockfile beside it. A separate lock and a separate advertisement are two records that can disagree, and disagreement between them is itself a split-brain source; worse, the advertisement is the record clients already trust, so a lock that clients do not read cannot protect them. Making the trusted record the claim keeps one authority.\n\nClaim mechanics. Attempt os.OpenFile(path, O_CREATE|O_EXCL|O_WRONLY, 0600). Success means this process owns the repository. This is portable, needs no new dependency and no platform-specific syscall — the tree currently has no flock anywhere, and golang.org/x/sys is only an indirect dependency.\n\nRepository-scoped identity, aliases and symlinks. The claim is scoped by the inode of MetaDir, because the path is derived from git rev-parse --git-common-dir (app.go:203-217, app.go:351). Two spellings of one repository — a symlinked parent, a relative path, a linked worktree — resolve to the same directory, so O_EXCL races on the same file even when the two path strings differ. Two genuinely different clones are different directories, so different repositories still run concurrently. The identity condition in the backlog item is met by the location, not by a computed key, and the genesis field already in the record (resident.go:15-19, checked at resident.go:57) guards the residual case where a directory is reused by a different workroom.\n\nLiveness and stale-owner recovery. On EEXIST, read the record and decide:\n- Genesis differs: the record belongs to a workroom this repository no longer has. Readers already refuse it (resident.go:57). Treat the claim as vacant and take over.\n- Genesis matches: probe the advertised URL over loopback with a short deadline.\n  - It answers and confirms the same genesis: the incumbent is alive. Refuse to serve. Exit non-zero with a message naming the incumbent's URL, so the operator or agent uses --server against it.\n  - The dial is refused, meaning nothing is listening on that address: the owner is gone. Take over by replacing the record with the existing temp-file-and-rename write.\n  - Anything else — a connection that opens but does not answer in time, an answer that does not parse, an answer naming a different genesis: refuse. Do not take over.\n\nThat asymmetry is the load-bearing part of the design and I want it attacked directly. Take over only on a definitive negative; never on ambiguity. A hung-but-listening incumbent still holds the port and may still hold client sessions, so evicting it on a timeout is precisely how a lease design manufactures the split it was built to prevent. Refusing on ambiguity is recoverable by a human in one step (stop the incumbent, or delete the record); taking over on ambiguity is not recoverable at all, because both processes then believe they are the owner.\n\nWhy probing beats a PID check. The record already carries PID (resident.go:18) and the withdrawal already compares it (resident.go:45). A PID is a weak liveness signal: it is meaningless across container and PID namespaces, where the number may name an unrelated process or nothing at all, and PID reuse produces a false \"alive\" that would wedge the repository permanently — the exact failure the backlog item names. Probing tests the thing that actually matters, whether anyone is serving that room, over the exact channel clients use.\n\nWhy no heartbeat or mtime lease. A lease adds a background writer, a wall-clock assumption, an expiry constant to tune, and a genuine split-brain window: an owner alive but paused past expiry is evicted while still serving. It buys automatic recovery from a hang, which the probe deliberately declines to do for the reason above. The probe answers the same question with less machinery and no clock.\n\nOwner token. Keep the withdrawal guard but stop keying it on PID. Write a random 16-byte owner nonce into the record and remove the file only when the nonce still matches. Same shape as resident.go:41-48, correct across namespaces and PID reuse, one extra field. PID stays as a diagnostic.\n\nHonest failure modes.\n- Simultaneous start is resolved by O_EXCL, which is atomic. The loser refuses. No window.\n- A crash leaves a claim, and the next start clears it after one refused dial. Nothing durable is deleted, so recovery satisfies \"recoverable without deleting durable work\".\n- A crashed owner whose port has since been taken by an unrelated process would answer the probe; the genesis check in the probe response is what stops that from reading as a live incumbent. This is why the probe must confirm genesis and not merely connect.\n- A firewall or sandbox that blackholes loopback turns a dead owner into an ambiguous one, and the resident refuses to start until the operator removes the record. Acceptable, and it must be in the refusal message.\n- Filesystems where O_EXCL is unreliable, notably some network filesystems, weaken the atomicity. The supported host is a local trusted workstation or container, so this is out of the supported envelope; worth stating in the docs rather than engineering around.\n\nSecond-resident behaviour: refuse. Not wait, not take over. Refusing generalises what the operating system already does on the default port today (main.go:1689) into deliberate behaviour on every address, which makes this a correction of an inconsistency rather than a new rule. Waiting would add supervisor semantics nobody has asked for; if it is ever wanted it is a --wait flag on top of the same claim, and I propose it explicitly as a non-goal now.\n\n== 6. OPTION (b): SHARED NEXUS OR LEADER ==\n\nWhat sharing would have to mean. Hub state is plain in-process maps behind one mutex (nexus.go:225-241), with a private ed25519 key (nexus.go:243-249) and a random generation (nexus.go:253-276). Sharing it requires one of two things, and both are large.\n\nMove the state to a shared transactional store. This contradicts the package's stated design — \"intentionally amnesiac\", cursor namespace dies with the process (nexus.go:1-3) — and forces the signing key to become durable so frames stay verifiable across residents, which moves a secret from process memory onto disk and widens exactly the key-storage exposure the supported-host decision names as inside the trust boundary. It also makes cursors and generations cluster-wide concepts.\n\nElect a leader and forward. Followers proxy the nexus endpoints to the leader. But electing a leader requires precisely the repository-scoped exclusive claim that option (a) is, so option (b) contains option (a) and then adds a forwarding layer, failover semantics, and the question of what happens to leases and cursors when the leader dies mid-session — which, given generation-scoped cursors, resets every client anyway. A follower is a proxy with extra hops and extra failure modes.\n\nWhat it would buy. The ability to run two residents for one repository. Under the adopted boundary that is one operator on one machine, so a second resident adds no availability (same failure domain), no scale (the load is one operator's agents, bounded already by MaxLiveSessions 256 at nexus.go:49), and no isolation (they are the same trust domain by stipulation). I can find no requirement it serves.\n\nI do not think (b) is wrong in principle. It is the right design for a world with several hosts or several trust domains, and if the deferred shared-host option in the supported-host decision is ever taken up, this is where it would be revisited. It is wrong now, for a defect whose entire content is that nothing says no.\n\n== 7. RECOMMENDATION ==\n\nOption (a). The reasoning a reviewer should attack, stated plainly:\n\nThe durable layer is already correct under concurrency, by CAS (kernel.go:480-541). The only thing multiple residents break is the ephemeral room, and the ephemeral room is deliberately process-local and amnesiac. So the choice is between making a deliberately amnesiac thing durable and shared, or making the documented \"run exactly one\" true. The second is smaller, it matches the fail-closed posture the supported-host decision adopted, and it removes an inconsistency the code already half-implements on the default port. The first pays a distributed-systems cost for a capability nobody has asked for and the trust model does not reward.\n\nThe strongest objection I can see, and I would rather the reviewer press it than let it pass: refusing removes an escape hatch. Someone who genuinely wants a second view of one repository can no longer get one by starting another resident. My answer is that they never had one — what they got was a silently forked room — and the supported path is to point clients at the incumbent with --server, which the CLI already requires. If that answer is wrong, (b) is the design, not a softer version of (a).\n\n== 8. PHASE-2 SHAPE ==\n\nBranch: request/multi-resident-coordination, as the request records.\n\nComponents touched:\n- internal/app/resident.go — ClaimResident replacing or wrapping PublishResident: O_EXCL claim, stale detection via probe, owner nonce in the record, nonce-guarded withdrawal. The Resident struct gains an owner field.\n- cmd/gs/main.go — serveCommand claims before it binds, or binds then claims and closes the listener on refusal; the refusal message names the incumbent URL and the recovery step. Refusal table in the docs gains a row.\n- internal/service/server.go — a cheap GET /v0/identity returning the workroom genesis and the nexus generation, so the probe can confirm which workroom answers a port. Existing endpoints either do not carry genesis or are too expensive for a startup probe.\n- internal/residentclient/client.go — the probe belongs here if it needs a client; this package is the one client-side resident boundary and already validates loopback origins (client.go:51-65).\n- docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md and docs/reference/gs/serve.md — \"Run exactly one. Nothing enforces it\" becomes a statement of what is now enforced, with the recovery step and the ambiguous-probe case.\n\nTakeover protocol, step by step:\n1. Resolve MetaDir from the git common directory. Attempt O_EXCL create of resident.json.\n2. On success: write URL, genesis, PID and a fresh random owner nonce; serve; on shutdown remove the file only if the nonce still matches.\n3. On EEXIST: read the record.\n4. Unparseable, or genesis differs: replace the claim and serve, recording why.\n5. Genesis matches: probe the advertised URL for identity with a short deadline.\n6. Identity confirms the same genesis: refuse, naming the incumbent URL. Exit non-zero. Nothing was bound.\n7. Dial refused: the owner is gone. Replace the record atomically and serve.\n8. Any other outcome: refuse, naming the record and how to clear it.\n\nRegression tests:\n- A second resident on a different port refuses while the first is live, and the first keeps its advertisement.\n- Simultaneous start of two residents: exactly one serves, one refuses, and the surviving record names the serving one.\n- Crashed owner recovery: a record whose address nothing is listening on is taken over, and the durable log is untouched.\n- Hung owner: a listener that accepts but never answers causes a refusal, not a takeover.\n- Port reused by a different workroom: identity mismatch causes a refusal to trust it as an incumbent while still allowing the claim to be replaced.\n- Graceful shutdown releases the claim; a successor then starts cleanly.\n- Path aliases and symlinks: the same repository reached by two path spellings, and via a linked worktree, contends for one claim; two distinct repositories serve concurrently.\n- The CAS invariant is untouched: the existing kernel concurrency tests keep passing unchanged, and no test in this change touches the sequencing path.\n- Wire parity and public surface gates for the new endpoint.\n\nArtifacts. Live artifacts already sit at internal/app (six), internal/nexus (two), cmd/gs (one), internal/service (one) and docs (one), so the successors go at those exact strings for whichever the merge actually changes, per the step-5 rules. One planning fact worth stating early: five of the six live internal/app artifacts, and the live cmd/gs and internal/service ones, were recorded by codex, and the fold admits a supersession only from the artifact's own author or an actor holding ratifier. Claude cannot retire them. The merge step will need codex or hugh, and since codex is the reviewer here that is convenient rather than blocking — but it should be agreed now, not discovered at merge time.\n\nAcceptance criteria for phase 2:\n- Starting a second resident for the same repository fails clearly before serving, or joins the same room; it never forks presence.\n- The claim is repository-scoped, crash-safe, and recoverable without deleting durable work.\n- Different repositories run concurrently.\n- Tests cover simultaneous start, stale-owner recovery, graceful shutdown, and path aliases and symlinks.\n- Deployment docs state the behaviour.\n- Full gates pass; independent exact-head review by codex.\n\n== 9. WHAT I AM ASKING THE REVIEWER TO DECIDE ==\n\n1. (a) or (b). If (b), say what requirement it serves that I have missed.\n2. Refuse, rather than wait or take over, as the second-resident behaviour.\n3. Take over only on a definitive negative probe, never on a timeout — the asymmetry in section 5.\n4. The claim living in resident.json rather than in a separate lockfile.\n5. Adding GET /v0/identity, an unauthenticated loopback endpoint returning genesis and nexus generation. Genesis is not secret — it appears in every event id — but it is a new surface and belongs in the security conclusion.\n\nNo implementation has begun. Phase 2 starts after this direction is approved."}